an emergency appeal by the united  nations has brought in pledges of more  than a billion dollars in aid for  afghanistan the un says the money is  needed to avert a humanitarian crisis in  the country following the taliban  takeover millions of people are said to  be on the verge of starvation the  pledges were paired with demands that  the taliban take steps to respect human  rights  the un says money would go to maintain  medical services water supply and  sanitation other projects in support of  women and children's education will also  receive funding  u.n chief antonio guterres says the  money will be a critical lifeline for  many afghans  the people of afghanistan need a  lifeline  after decades of war suffering and  insecurity  they face perhaps their most perilous  hour  now is the time for the international  community to stand with them  today  one in three afghans do not know where  their next meal will come from  the poverty rate is spiraling and basic  public services are close to collapse  even before the taliban returned to  power the country relied heavily on  international support but now life has  become much worse and many people lack  food and sufficient medical care  we can speak now to linda tom she's with  the united nations in kabul linda thank  you for joining us so tell us what the  biggest challenges are at the moment  um today in afghanistan we are on the  brink of a humanitarian catastrophe this  is due to 40 years of conflict for  current natural disasters and now we're  experiencing the second drought in four  years  that combined with a looming economic  crisis has been a deadly combination for  people in afghanistan  even before  the last couple of months of turmoil  already at the beginning of the year  half the population that's 18 million  people were in need of humanitarian  assistance in afghanistan  it is a dire situation we know that the  united nations has just secured a  billion dollars in aid pledges but  that's for a population of well over 30  million people who are facing as you  just said an economic crisis drought  many other factors  can that money make a difference  of course that money can make a  difference and i wanted to say that in  the past couple of weeks aid workers  are doing the best they can they are  working very hard in order to deliver  assistance to people in need so just in  the past couple of days we've seen um  tens of metric tons of food medicine  arriving in afghanistan  to be deployed immediately to hospitals  and to people who need assistance and  aid workers are working in order to make  sure that assistance gets to people in  need  and what are you hearing from un aid  workers on the ground at the moment what  is the greatest need that they are  assisting with is it food supplies is it  medical supplies  well all these areas are extremely  critical but as the world food program  has said that they're at risk of running  out of food in the next couple of weeks  and  the world health organization has also  said that while they are receiving  supplies of medicine much much more is  needed because of the  large amount of people who need  humanitarian assistance um many people  have been displaced this year so just  this year 600 000 people have been  displaced so they need shelter um like  all of us they just need a safe place to  live and they need their children to be  able to go to school um and um in some  cases it's just even basic things like  water and sanitation among these immense  needs linda are you worried about or aid  arriving where it should arrive under  this current taliban regime  well as i just highlighted you know  we're um we're it's it's a very good  sign that we're able to continue to get  assistance coming in by air but also by  road so um that flow of humanitarian  assistance needs to continue but one of  the issues that we were facing is that  we were running out of funding um so it  was really important that we get renewed  fundings and and um that the pledges um  are uh come in as quickly as possible so  that we can turn those pledges and that  money into medicine into food  and into actual supplies that can help  people on the ground right now  linda tom with the un office for the  coordination of humanitarian affairs  joining us there from kabul thank you  very much  thank you so much for having me  let's take a look at some of the latest  developments in afghanistan right now  the taliban have denied that one of  their top leaders mula abdulgani baradar  was killed in a shootout with rivals  that statement follows rumors about  splits within the movement a u.s  secretary of state anthony blinken has  defended the biden administration's  withdrawal from afghanistan lincoln  insisted to the u.s congress that  officials had prepared for the worst  case scenario and an international  journalists group says the return of the  taliban is a catastrophe for journalism  in afghanistan which is in danger of  disappearing from the country  the german military evacuated more than  5 000 people from kabul after the city  fell to the taliban but more than 10 000  people were still on the foreign  ministry's evacuation list when the  airlift ended on august 27th most of  them afghan citizens they include  journalists human rights activists and  others who could face taliban reprisals  if they remain in the country many are  still waiting for germany to offer them  secure ways out  it is a nerve-wracking wait also for  their afghan relatives already in  germany in berlin we met nargis a  student who was trying to get german  politicians not to look the other way  nagis carries money fundraised for  afghanistan by her school in berlin  the 20 year old afghan came to germany  with her parents and siblings five years  ago they fled the taliban  but some of her relatives are still  stuck in afghanistan  she worries most for her aunt a  politician who is now in hiding in kabul  suicide  she doesn't leave the 